  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nAbove normal temperatures and dry conditions are the name of the\ngame this week. Highs are forecast to be in the 70sF today with\ncalm conditions.\n\nMonday looks to be hot and breezy. A dry cold front is expected \nto move through the region, bringing westerly winds gusting in the\n20s to 30s mph. The highest gusts are expected in the mountains \nand foothills west of Billings, as well as southeastern Montana. \nHigh temperatures are forecast to be well above normals (60s and \n70sF), in the 80s to almost 90F. The warmest temperatures are \nforecast for the river valleys southeast of Billings, including \nSheridan, where there is a 30 to 60 percent chance of temperatures\nreaching or exceeding 90F. Currently three of the five climate \nsites could experience a record high temperature. Those three \nsites are Billings, Livingston, and Sheridan.\n\nIn addition to very warm temperatures and breezy conditions,\nminimum relative humidity values will drop into the teens percent\nMonday afternoon. Little precipitation has fallen since the\nSeptember event, especially for portions of southern and south-\ncentral Montana. As a result, a Fire Weather Watch has been issued\nfrom 12 PM MDT until 8 PM MDT Monday. Please take care not to\ncause a spark!\n\nThe rest of the week temperatures will remain above normal but not\nnearly as warm as Monday. Another dry cold front looks to pass\nthrough Monday night, bringing high temperatures down into the 70s\nand low 80sF Tuesday. Expect these temperatures to continue along\nwith dry conditions, resulting in elevated fire weather concerns\nthe rest of the week.\n\nEnsembles are hinting at a pattern change this weekend, with an\nupper trough impacting the region. The latest meteograms depict\nsignificantly cooler temperatures, perhaps well below normal by\nnext Sunday. Continue to monitor the forecast if you have plans\nnext weekend!\n\nMatos",
